WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.220
The Falklands sit where the cold,

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:05.732
nutrient-rich Falkland Current meets the South American shelf.

00:00:05.732 --> 00:00:08.849
The sea is extraordinarily productive; the land is not.

00:00:09.949 --> 00:00:13.496
The islands hold most of the world's black-browed albatrosses,

00:00:13.496 --> 00:00:17.445
commonly estimated at about seventy percent of the global population.

00:00:17.445 --> 00:00:21.222
This one nests on New Island, the site of this unit's archaeology.

00:00:22.322 --> 00:00:26.389
Gentoo, rockhopper and Magellanic penguins breed in large colonies,

00:00:26.389 --> 00:00:28.696
with king penguins at Volunteer Point.

00:00:29.796 --> 00:00:32.448
The only native land mammal was the warrah,

00:00:32.448 --> 00:00:35.531
a fox-like canid that Darwin found strangely tame.

00:00:35.531 --> 00:00:37.751
It was hunted to extinction by 1876.

00:00:37.751 --> 00:00:42.253
How it reached the islands is one of the puzzles in this unit's readings.

00:00:43.353 --> 00:00:46.866
Expeditions recorded the coasts before the sheep arrived.

00:00:46.866 --> 00:00:51.427
James Clark Ross's Antarctic expedition wintered in the Falklands in 1842,

00:00:51.427 --> 00:00:55.927
and its naturalists drew the towering tussac grass on its peat pedestals.

00:00:57.027 --> 00:01:01.848
Since the nineteenth century, sheep grazing has destroyed most of the original tussac

00:01:01.848 --> 00:01:04.003
grass, which once fringed every coast.

00:01:04.003 --> 00:01:07.746
Rats and cats have cleared ground-nesting birds from many islands.

00:01:07.746 --> 00:01:11.830
Restoration now focuses on the outer islands that are free of predators.
